                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V//G \to \mathbf{B}G
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coCartesian+fibration"} coCartesian fibration
            arises this way, up to equivalence.
          %h3#Integralreal Integral versus real cohomology
          %p
            One of the most basic fibration sequences that appears all over the place in practice is the sequence of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-MacLane+object"} Eilenberg-MacLane object
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi n
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi n
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi n
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
